<article class="env-lemma" id="04MG">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04MG">Lemma <span data-tag="04MG">9.6.3.8</span>.</a> Let $\lambda $ be a regular cardinal and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category which admits $\lambda $-small filtered colimits. Let $U$ be a collection of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ with the following property: for every object $Y \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, there exists a morphism $u: Y \rightarrow Z$ which belongs to $U$. Then, for every object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and every ordinal $\alpha &lt; \lambda $, there exists a diagram $F: \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{\leq \alpha } ) \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ satisfying the following conditions: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(a)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The object $F(0)$ coincides with $X$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(b)$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every ordinal $\alpha &lt; \lambda $, the morphism $F(\alpha ) \rightarrow F(\alpha +1)$ belongs to $U$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(c)$</dt>
<dd><p>
For every nonzero limit ordinal $\alpha \leq \lambda $, the restriction $F|_{ \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{\leq \alpha }) }$ is a colimit diagram. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $Q$ denote the collection of all diagrams $F_{\beta }: \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{\leq \beta } ) \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ satisfying conditions $(a)$, $(b)$, and $(c)$, where $\beta $ is an ordinal $\leq \alpha $. We regard $Q$ as a partially ordered set, where $F_{\beta } \leq F_{\beta '}$ if $\beta \leq \beta '$ and $F_{\beta } = F_{\beta '} |_{ \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{\leq \beta } ) }$. Note that $Q$ is nonempty: it has a least element given by the diagram $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{\leq 0} ) \simeq \{ X\}  \hookrightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ taking the value $X$. We claim that $Q$ satisfies the hypothesis of Zorn's lemma: that is, every linearly ordered set $Q' \subset Q$ admits an upper bound. Without loss of generality, we may assume that $Q'$ is nonempty and has no largest element. In this case, the elements of $Q'$ can be amalgamated to a diagram $F_{ &lt; \gamma }: \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{ &lt; \gamma } ) \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, where $\gamma \leq \alpha $ is a nonzero limit ordinal. Our assumption on $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ then guarantees that $F_{ &lt; \gamma }$ can be extended to a colimit diagram 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[ F_{\leq \gamma }: \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{\leq \gamma } ) \simeq \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{ &lt; \gamma } )^{\triangleright } \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}. \]
</div>
<p>
       By construction, this diagram satisfies conditions $(a)$, $(b)$, and $(c)$, and is therefore an upper bound for $Q'$. 
    <p>
      Applying Zorn's lemma, we deduce that $Q$ has a maximal element $F_{\beta }: \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{\leq \beta } ) \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. We will complete the proof by showing that $\beta = \alpha $. Assume otherwise, and set $Y = F_{\beta }(\beta )$. By assumption, we can choose a morphism $u: Y \rightarrow Z$ which belongs to $U$. Let us identify $u$ with an object $\widetilde{Z}$ of the coslice $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{Y/}$. Since the inclusion map $\{  \beta + 1\}  \hookrightarrow \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{\leq \beta + 1} )$ is right anodyne (Example <a data-tag="02LJ" href="/tag/02LJ">4.3.7.11</a>), the restriction map $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ F_{\beta } / } \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ Y/ }$ is a trivial Kan fibration (Corollary <a data-tag="018L" href="/tag/018L">4.3.6.14</a>). We can therefore lift $\widetilde{Z}$ to an object of the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ F_{\beta } / }$, which we can identify with an extension of $F_{\beta }$ to a diagram $F_{\beta +1}: \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }( \mathrm{Ord}_{ \leq \beta +1 }) \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. By construction, this diagram carries the pair $(\beta , \beta +1)$ to the morphism $u$ of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. It follows that $F_{\beta +1}$ is also an element of $Q$, contradicting the maximality of $F_{\beta }$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></p></article>
